@startuml SandboxBackend
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Sandbox backend — the only backend: sbx runs the agent, or the run refuses
' ── DECIDED 2026-10-16 (Executor): sbx OR REFUSE ───────────────────────────
' The Executor chose, in order: "Retire Docker egress"; "Refuse without sbx";
' "Retire review, map the rest"; on the tier mapping: "Nothing, `sbx` handles
' this. Drop that param". The docker+egress backend (internal/backend/
' dockeregress, internal/egress, internal/egressproxy, internal/egresspolicy,
' internal/broker, internal/reviewgate, cmd/proveo-egress, defs/sidecars,
' defs/egress-dashboard) is DELETED. There is no Docker fallback and no host
' fallback. The host-execution decision space stays DEFERRED in
' _spec/_plans/host-execution-fallback.puml.
'
' MEASURED, internal/run/run.go requireSandbox(p, d) (:94-99), before any
' workspace work:
'   sbx not ready (sandbox.Ready → sbx.Available, or Ensure off --print)
'     → sandbox.Unavailable(why): the reason, the engine, and the install or
'       upgrade line from sbx.InstallCmd
' (F10, 2026-10-17: the manifest no longer has a `docker:` key — Manifest.Docker,
' DockerMode and IsSbx are deleted, and the decoder's KnownFields(true) makes a
' stale `docker: sbx` fail loading with its name — so the "manifest lacks
' `docker: sbx`" refusal is gone with the key; every harness runs in sbx by
' construction. _spec/internal/manifest/harness-manifest-schema.puml.)
' `--print` refuses too: it calls sbx.Available and never installs.
' e2e/sbx_test.go pins the refusal (TestSandboxBackendRefusesWhenSbxAbsent).
'
' ── THE RUN DIR (N13, DONE (wt) 2026-10-17) ───────────────────────────────
' MEASURED: sandbox.Input.RunDir (sandbox.go:1036; was EgDir) is
' <StateDir>/runs/<sid> (run.RunDirOf). Spec renders the Kit at
' <RunDir>/sbx/kit (:1286); PrepareHomeAccess, seedProjectEnv,
' CapturePolicyLog(runDir, name) and CaptureMemoryEvidence(runDir, name)
' (:1446, :1461, :1615-1616) all write under it. The e2e globs read
' <state>/runs/*/sbx/... (e2e/sbx_test.go:443, sbx_egress_test.go:136).
'
' ── NO RETIREMENT STUBS (DECIDED 2026-10-17) ───────────────────────────────
' The user, on the CLI and env stubs: "Drop all four" (--egress-mode, --shell,
' PROVEO_SBX off values, PROVEO_DIND). MEASURED: sandbox.Disabled, the
' PROVEO_SBX off-value refusal and TestRetiredSbxOffValuesRefuseTheRun are
' deleted; warnDindRetired and its PROVEO_DIND warning are deleted; the hidden
' `--egress-mode` flag is deleted. PROVEO_SBX and PROVEO_DIND are now ordinary
' unread variables, and `--egress-mode` / `--shell` get cobra's "unknown flag".
' HISTORY: from 2026-10-16 to this decision, PROVEO_SBX off values hard-failed
' ("PROVEO_SBX=<v> is retired: …") and PROVEO_DIND warned and no-opped.
'
' ── `--shell` IS RETIRED: SHELL ACCESS IS sbx's (DECIDED 2026-10-17) ───────
' The user: "Why do we support this? Does any other harness support that
' --shell flag? is that `proveo` or `sbx`? if `proveo` retire it." It was
' proveo's, so it is gone with no stub: Params.Shell, sandbox.Input.Shell and
' its branches in sandbox.go and opencode_cache.go are deleted (MEASURED). A
' shell inside a run's sandbox is `sbx ls`, then `sbx exec -it <sandbox> bash`.
' The golden sbx-claudecode-shell-clone-datadir became
' sbx-claudecode-clone-datadir, and its agent is `claude`.
'
' ── A STOP TEARS DOWN NORMALLY AND EXITS 128+sig (DECIDED 2026-10-17) ──────
' The user, on how SIGTERM/SIGHUP should end a run: "Stop runs normal
' teardown"; then, on its exit status: "Exit 128+sig, 'stopped'
' (Recommended)". MEASURED: stopOutcome(runErr, sig, forced) returns
' (teardown nil, result backend.Stopped{sig}) for a forwarded signal that is
' not the Ctrl+C "remove" path. A closed terminal or `kill -TERM proveo` prints
' "stopped by SIGTERM — ending as on a normal exit", lifts clone edits and
' commits, saves state and runs `sbx rm` (an OpenCode engine is retained), with
' no template-reload retry. proveo then EXITS 143 (SIGTERM) or 129 (SIGHUP)
' through cmd/proveo exitCode, and the transcript reads "outcome: stopped by
' SIGTERM". A SIGTERM no longer looks like success to a caller.
' HISTORY: before, a stop read as a failed run ("kept for diagnosis", no
' `sbx rm`); an earlier revision on 2026-10-17 exited 0.
' Teardown still keeps the sandbox when clone work could not be carried home.
' Lifecycle: _spec/internal/sbx/kit-lifecycle.puml; exit status:
' _spec/internal/backend/exit-code.puml; the Ctrl+C path:
' _spec/internal/ptyproxy/pty-ownership.puml.
' A failed run's kept-for-diagnosis hint names `sbx exec -it <name> bash`, as
' in sbx's own exec reference (https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/sbx/exec/).
'
' sandbox.Input.Workdir and run.Spec's Workspace.Workdir are deleted: nothing
' read them (MEASURED). The agent's working directory reaches the sandbox as
' PROVEO_WORKDIR, computed from the binds by agentWorkdir (see the CLI note
' below). MountSpec.Plan() no longer returns a workdir at all: since 2026-10-17
' (F20, the user: "Drop the workdir return (Recommended)") it returns
' (mounts []runner.Mount, links []Link) (MEASURED, internal/workspace/mount.go:56;
' internal/run/run.go:395). The dropped value was the constant "/app" (reported).
'
' THE IMAGE READS NO WORKSPACE .env; THE HOST SEEDS ITS VALUES (N7, DONE (wt)
' 2026-10-17; the user: "Keep 1+1, seed --env-file (Recommended)"). MEASURED:
' the bash find_env_file and load_env and Go entrypoint FindEnvFile,
' LoadEnvFile and ShouldSkipEnvLoad are deleted; what was left of load_env is
' bridge_google_keys (the Google key aliases only), and proveo-entrypoint prep
' has no .env branch (since N1/N22 it keeps only the passwd entry and the
' git-context report, and the shell prelude runs before it —
' _spec/cmd/proveo-entrypoint/prep-sequence.puml). So no image sources the workspace .env, inside
' sbx or out, and there is no guard left to key on SANDBOX_NAME / SANDBOX_VM_ID
' (repo-simplification.puml F7, resolved by N7). Pinned by
' internal/contract/env_prelude_test.go TestNeitherPreludeReadsTheWorkspaceEnv
' and TestBashPreludeBridgesTheGoogleKeyAliases. The project .env's values reach
' a NEW sandbox through sbx's `--env-file` (sbx accepts it since v0.39.0,
' reported; the floor is 0.42.0): _spec/internal/credentials/
' credential-decisions.puml, PROJECT .env. The agent can still READ a .env in
' the mounted tree, and credentials.WarnMountedSecrets(dir, lookup) says so when
' it holds a provider key. The broker sentinel (apply_broker_sentinel,
' ApplyBrokerSentinel, "proveo-brokered", PROVEO_CREDENTIAL_BROKER_KEYS) and
' CanonicalTiers are deleted.
' HISTORY (2026-10-16 → N7): the guard skipped sourcing when SANDBOX_NAME or
' SANDBOX_VM_ID was non-blank (before that, only while PROVEO_EGRESS_MODE named
' a tier), and its bash form had to read both through `:-` defaults to survive
' `set -u` — an image test with SANDBOX_NAME unset once died "SANDBOX_NAME:
' unbound variable".
' ONE sbx DETECTOR (F18, DONE (wt) 2026-10-17). MEASURED: _proveo_in_sbx checks
' SANDBOX_NAME, then SANDBOX_ID, then SANDBOX_VM_ID, each through a `:-`
' default so it survives `set -u` — in packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh
' (:2668-2669, used by proveo_sbx_passthrough) and as a POSIX copy in
' packages/lib/proveo-await-seed (:12-13, used by the seed wait and the refusal
' check). sbx v0.39.0 documents SANDBOX_NAME and SANDBOX_ID as a sandbox's
' identity (reported); whether SANDBOX_VM_ID is deprecated is [Unverified], so it
' stays the last fallback. Pinned by TestBothSbxSitesDetectSbxByAnyIdentityVariable
' (internal/contract/sbx_passthrough_test.go).
'
' inject/broker.env IS NO LONGER WRITTEN. assembleEnv writes no file; no proxy
' of proveo's needs a key. secret-custody (d) is closed, not moved.
'
' IMAGE PREFLIGHT LIVES IN launchSandbox. MEASURED: it runs only when
' !p.PrintOnly && d.PreflightImages != nil && !sbx.HasTemplate(p.Image), and
' Deps.PreflightImages(man, agentImage) ensures the agent image alone — there
' are no sidecar images, and the egress-proxy image inspect and its "egress
' proxy: … (local build …)" header are deleted.
'
' ── THE AGENT ENVIRONMENT, AFTER THE PLAN WENT ─────────────────────────────
' HISTORY: from 2026-09-15 `egress.AgentEnv` carried the docker plan's agent
' environment (the local-model set, PROVEO_EGRESS_MODE) into cfg.Env, because
' the sandbox path had dropped it and `--local-model` did nothing under sbx.
' internal/egress is deleted. MEASURED now: run.launchSandbox builds
' `sandbox.Input.AgentEnv` itself — host CDP and adb add-on env, operator.Env,
' and sbx.LocalModelEnv(model) plus SBX_CRED_*_MODE=none for a host Ollama run
' (_spec/internal/sbx/host-inference.puml). No tier variable crosses.
'
' ── THE AGENT HOLDS AT LAUNCH WHAT THE HOST RESOLVED ───────────────────────
' MEASURED 2026-09-15/16, sbx 0.42.1: sbx
' does NOT gate the agent launch on the Kit's startup commands. The interactive
' shell is handed over at t≈0 while the dispatcher reports `dispatcher complete`
' at t+15–30s, and the schema offers no blocking form (`KitCommand` is
' command/user/description). So the def entrypoint's role under sbx is SPLIT:
' anything the agent must HOLD at launch — environment, config file, git index,
' safe.directory — is proveo's to render HOST-SIDE into the kit environment;
' anything that may happen AFTER launch — seed composition, house rules — may
' stay a startup command. `launchEnv` is where the first half is assembled, and
' `Input.AgentEnv` is simply its first entry.
'   what the agent holds at launch .. _spec/internal/sbx/launch-env.puml
'
' ── AND THE SANDBOX DROPS THE PAIRS THAT NAME A PROXY IT HAS NOT GOT ───────
' `sandboxAgentEnv` (via proxyOnlyVar) filters HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY, NO_PROXY
' (and the lower-case forms), NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS, CURL_CA_BUNDLE,
' REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE, SSL_CERT_FILE and GIT_SSL_CAINFO (MEASURED). sbx enforces
' the policy and terminates TLS itself, so a CA bundle or proxy URL that does
' not name sbx's own proxy would black-hole every request. INSPECT_PROXY,
' ENFORCEMENT_PROXY and PROVEO_EGRESS_CA_CERT left the list 2026-10-16: their
' only producer, the docker egress plan, is deleted (repo-simplification.puml F8).
'
' HISTORY: the mode handed to BuildPlan here was best-effort, because `p.Mode`
' could hold an sbx baseline. BuildPlan and `p.Mode` are both gone.
'
' ── ONE SANDBOX PER DEF PER WORKSPACE, NOT ONE PER RUN ─────────────────────
' Measured 2026-09-15: 81 sandboxes on one developer host after a few e2e suite
' runs, 4 of them still running. The name was the SESSION id, so every run
' created a VM, seeded it, and abandoned it — paying full creation cost for a
' sandbox identical to the one it had just discarded, and leaving the discard
' for the operator to find.
'
' `sbx run --name` re-attaches to an existing sandbox and reads the agent and
' workspace from its spec, so the fix is the NAME: proveo-<target>-<fingerprint
' of the workspace path>. Keyed on the def alone, a second project would have
' silently inherited the first project's mounts. See clone-workspace.puml for
' why the ref namespace does NOT follow this key.
'
' The golden pins the name TWICE, and one assertion alone would not have held.
' It scrubs the fingerprint to `<WORKHASH>`, because the workspace is a fresh
' `t.TempDir()` on every run and the raw hash would rewrite the golden each
' time — and then calls `Spec` a second time over the same input to assert the
' two answers agree. Scrubbing without that second call is precisely how a
' per-run name would go on passing.
'
' Reuse does not pay everywhere. The e2e suite gives each case its own workspace,
' so def+workspace keying buys it nothing while sbx still sizes every sandbox at
' half the host; the sweep that keeps that from filling the machine, and the OOM
' that forced it, are in _spec/tests/testing-strategy.puml.
'
' purpose: how a run reaches the sbx backend, what it hands over, and what it refuses
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/**
' covers: internal/sbx/sbx.go
' covers: internal/sbx/exec.go
' covers: internal/sbx/run.go
' covers: internal/sbx/agents.go
' covers: internal/sbx/limits.go
' covers: internal/run/run.go (requireSandbox, launchSandbox)
' covers: internal/entrypoint/entrypoint.go (the prelude, which reads no .env)
' covers: packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh (bridge_google_keys, _proveo_in_sbx, proveo_sbx_passthrough)
' covers: packages/lib/proveo-await-seed (_proveo_in_sbx)
' covers: internal/contract/env_prelude_test.go
' covers: internal/contract/await_seed_sbx_test.go (TestAwaitSeedDetectsSbxByAnyIdentityVariable)
' covers: e2e/sbx_test.go (the refusal tests)
' covers: e2e/idle_test.go
' covers: cmd/proveo/golden_test.go (TestSandboxPlanGolden)
' covers: internal/credentials/credentials.go (AgentTranscript, agentTranscriptDirs)
'   the MCP gateway it declines .. _spec/internal/sbx/mcp-gateway.puml
'   what the agent holds at launch _spec/internal/sbx/launch-env.puml
'   what decides egress ........... _spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline.puml
'   who holds the key ............. _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml
'   the deferred host fallback .... _spec/_plans/host-execution-fallback.puml
'
' ── WHERE THE FAILURE PATH LOOKS FOR THE AGENT'S TRANSCRIPT ────────────────
' credentials.AgentTranscript walks one root per target under the proveo home
' (agentTranscriptDirs): claudecode `.claude/projects`, codex `.codex/sessions`.
' codex files one rollout .jsonl per session under a date tree,
' $CODEX_HOME/sessions/YYYY/MM/DD/rollout-<stamp>-<uuid>.jsonl. The walk
' recurses and matches the `.jsonl` suffix, so the table names only the root
' and never enumerates the dates. A target with no root names no transcript.
'
' ── THE IDLE PROBE: THE ONE CASE THAT TYPES NOTHING ─────────────────────────
' Every other e2e drives the agent. `e2e/idle_test.go` reaches a prompt and then
' does NOTHING, longer than any timer in the stack, and asserts the agent is
' still there — input is the variable being held at zero. sbx's auto-stop fires
' 30s after the session DISCONNECTS, so the default 5-minute wait clears it by an
' order of magnitude. It spends no model call: an agent sitting at its prompt has
' not been asked anything. Gated behind PROVEO_IDLE_TEST=1 because it waits on
' purpose (~6 minutes), with PROVEO_IDLE_FOR / PROVEO_IDLE_STARTUP as the knobs.
'
' It needs a REAL pty (`pty.Start`), not tmux: `sbx run -t <image> shell <ws>`
' exits within seconds in a detached pane with proveo uninvolved (see
' e2e/sbx_test.go). The pty is sized 50x200 first — a TUI that cannot read the
' window size draws one character per line and the banner never appears in a
' recognisable form.
'
' ── FOUR VERDICTS, NOT TWO, AND THE READINGS THAT COST TIME ─────────────────
' "the agent is gone" is only one of the ways this run ends, and reporting the
' others as failures sends the reader after the wrong thing:
'   SKIP    an auth failure — "Failed to authenticate", "Credit balance is too
'           low", "Please run /login", "Not logged in", cursor-agent's "Press any
'           key to log in". A session that could not start proves nothing either
'           way. Claude Code reports "Not logged in" in the STATUS BAR and then
'           exits, which is exactly the failure this suite must distinguish from
'           a session stopped underneath a HEALTHY agent.
'   BLOCKED a screen that WAITS for a human — Claude Code's first-run theme
'           picker ("Choose the text style", "Let's get started"), which appears
'           whenever HOME names a path with no config in it. Alive, but it can
'           never proceed unattended: a different fault from dying.
'   ALIVE   the harness's OWN TUI frame. "reached a prompt" is not a shared
'           shape, so there is one marker row per target (cursor · opencode ·
'           cecli · claudecode). Matching Claude Code's frame against a cursor
'           session reported a healthy agent as a hang for 481 seconds — a test
'           failing rather than a def failing.
'   DEAD    "was stopped" · "kept for diagnosis" · "agent exited with code".
'
' The markers are matched against ANSI-stripped text, and that is not cosmetic:
' the status bar is laid out with cursor-positioning escapes BETWEEN words, so
' "Not logged in" reaches the buffer as "Not\x1b[177Glogged\x1b[184Gin" and every
' plain substring match for it silently fails.
'
' Prompt detection reads the TUI's own frame rather than proveo's entrypoint
' banner: the banner is printed BEFORE the agent starts, so a session can be
' fully up without it being the last thing said — and the stock sbx image never
' prints it, which the ladder's rung 0 needs.
'
' PROVEO_IDLE_TARGETS takes a comma-separated list (default: claudecode alone).
' Sweeping matters because this failure has only ever been reported on ONE
' harness at a time, and a per-harness answer is what separates "this agent
' exits" from "sbx stops sessions". The ladder holds a raw `sbx run`; idle
' holds `proveo run`, which is the ptyproxy hop a leaked DA1 becomes a prompt
' on. `mise run test-e2e-full` is the gate that sweeps all four defs at 45s
' (past sbx's 30s auto-stop and the ~14s cursor death). A 5-minute wait is
' still the thorough "operator stepped away" check. Cursor 2026.09.15 is the
' open case that died at a healthy TUI — see
' _spec/internal/ptyproxy/terminal-report-filter.puml.
'
' The idle PTY does not answer Device Attributes and an empty TempDir never
' hits the go.mod+package.json seed path, so a "ZERO input on an empty dir"
' wait used to PASS while `proveo run cursor` against this repo died at ~16s.
' idle_test.go now writes that workspace shape and, once the prompt is up,
' injects kitty-push, DA1 (complete, split, orphan, 8-bit CSI). The filter
' must drop every burst; a leak is a prompt nobody typed.
'
' ── Authoritative sbx references. The Kit spec is the source of truth for every
' field below. Deriving the shape from sbx's rejection messages instead produced a
' Kit that validated while omitting what actually makes an agent session work.
'   kit spec v2 : https://github.com/docker/sbx-kits-contrib/blob/main/spec/SPEC-v2.md
'   kit examples: https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/customize/kit-examples/
'   spec package: https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/docker/sbx-kits-contrib/spec
'   contrib kits: https://github.com/docker/sbx-kits-contrib
' Kit schema, the validate handoff, session lifecycle and exit-code handling:
'   sbx-kit-contract.puml (same directory)

component "run.Do → requireSandbox(p, d)\n""sandbox.Ready"" false → refuse" as SEL <<app>>
component "internal/sbx\nAvailable · WriteKit · SecretSet\nRunArgs · RemoveArgs" as SBX <<app>>
component "sbx CLI (pre-GA; proveo pins\n""sbx.MinVersion"")\nrun --kit … / secret set / rm" as CLI <<host>>
component "Kit spec.yaml (per run)\nnetwork.allow = add-on ports (Ollama ·\nCDP · adb) ∪ own-agent credential domains" as KIT <<app>>
component "host-side credential injection\nvalues via stdin — never argv,\nnever env, never the VM fs" as SEC <<error>>

component "refusal\n""sandbox.Unavailable(why)""\ninstall / upgrade hint" as REFUSE <<error>>

SEL ARROW_MAIN SBX : ready
SEL ARROW_ERROR REFUSE : sbx not ready
SBX ARROW_MAIN CLI
SBX ..> KIT : rendered under <state>/runs/<sid>/sbx/kit
SBX ..> SEC

note right of SEL
  **No fallback, no override.** Every harness runs in sbx — the manifest no
  longer carries a ""docker:"" key (F10, 2026-10-17) — and sbx is the only
  backend (DECIDED 2026-10-16: "Refuse without sbx"). The
  choice prompt carries it as a COMPULSORY ""docker (sandbox)"" — greyed AND
  ticked, help "Mount the current git project in a sandbox for the agent". A
  remembered 'off' does not survive the prompt: the gate re-ticks the box and
  still reports it as selected
  (""_spec/internal/choiceui/choice-prompt-render\.puml""). A host without sbx
  (no CLI, no Apple Silicon or ""/dev/kvm"", too old) refuses the run with the
  reason and the install line. ""--print"" refuses the same way.
end note

note right of SBX
  **Availability gate** (all must hold):
  | sbx CLI on PATH, at or above ""sbx.MinVersion""
  | darwin/arm64, **or** linux with ""/dev/kvm""
  Win11/Hyper-V and Intel macs are not gated in yet.
end note

note bottom of SEC
  **sbx is the broker.** Declared secret env vars plus every permitted
  provider key var go to ""sbx secret set"" before the run. proveo's broker
  sentinel, ""broker.env"" and the MITM that read it were DELETED 2026-10-16.
  Accepted gap: no DLP content scan on payloads to hosts sbx's policy allows.
end note

note bottom of SEL
  **A Kit's credentials are approved by the operator, and that is a gate rather
  than a fault.** sbx lists every credential a Kit requests — service,
  destination, whether it is stored — and waits on //"[A]pprove all · [R]eview
  each · [N]o"//. A ""proveo run"" is interactive by construction (the choice form
  precedes it, ""isStdinTTY"" gates the prompts, the agent needs a PTY), so this
  sits naturally in front of it and the e2e probe answers it the way an operator
  would.

  //An earlier note here called it a blocker.// That conflated two different
  things. ""secret set"" asking "Overwrite? (y/N)" IS a fault, because the value is
  piped on stdin and the question consumes it — the write cancels and the agent
  keeps a stale credential, silently. That is what ""--force"" fixes, and the probe
  still fails on it. Being asked a question is not the same as having an answer
  stolen.

  It does raise the cost of over-declaring, though. Provider collection is
  filtered by the manifest's ""capabilities.providers"": unfiltered, a claudecode
  sandbox asked for cursor, google, openai and xai keys, so the operator was being
  asked to approve reach that harness has no way to exercise.
end note

note left of REFUSE
  **HISTORY — the fallback contract, 2026-09 to 2026-10-16.** A run landed on
  docker+egress when the gate failed, or on ""PROVEO_SBX=0"" or
  ""--egress-mode review"". From 2026-09-15 that path still shipped but was no
  longer tested: its e2e cases skipped through ""skipOutsideSbx"". The
  2026-10-16 decision deleted the path and its tests, and
  ""TestSandboxBackendFallsBackToDockerWhenSbxAbsent"" became
  ""TestSandboxBackendRefusesWhenSbxAbsent"": a refused run must not render or
  promise a docker fallback.
end note

note bottom of CLI
  **Plan vs proof, and the proof had to be made cheap.** The print-only tests
  assert the ARGV from pure ""sandbox.Spec"" with no VM.
  ""TestEveryHarnessGetsTheDockerAccessItPromises"" runs the harness for real on
  this backend and asserts what the sandbox promises (the ""docker: sbx"" key is
  gone with F10; ""WantsDocker"" reads ""daemon:""): the sbx line was printed,
  the workspace mount carries writes back to the host, and the
  sandbox is gone afterwards. It drives the harness's own kit launch and probes
  through ""sbx exec"" (""launchKit"", e2e/kit_probe_test.go), so it asks the agent
  nothing; it used ""--shell"" until that flag was retired 2026-10-17.

  **That test alone was not enough, and the gap is instructive.** Every adapter
  defect — a rejected ""-w"", a ""-v"" that is ""--cloud"" only, an image in the agent
  positional, an agent name that must match the Kit's, an unparseable Kit, a stale
  template served silently — was found by running ""proveo run"" BY HAND. The real
  run failed at each of them but reported only //"session exited before the
  sandbox shell"// six minutes later, naming none. Three assertions close that:

  | ""TestSandboxKitValidatesAgainstTheRealCLI"" | renders the Kit and hands it to ""sbx kit validate"" — catches schema drift in ~2s with no VM, and treats a DEPRECATION warning as failure because that is sbx announcing the schema moved |
  | the run probe | fails on sbx's own ""ERROR:"" line, and on an interactive prompt, instead of waiting out the clock |

  //A third row, ""TestSandboxKitDeclaresOnlyPermittedProviders"", no longer
  exists in e2e/ (MEASURED 2026-10-16).// The permitted-provider filter is
  pinned a layer down: ""internal/backend/sandbox/provider_gate_test\.go"".

  A fast pass is where a vacuous assertion hides, so the validator was checked
  against a deliberately broken Kit: at ""schemaVersion: 1"" it fails in 1.7s naming
  //"field permissions not found in type spec\.SpecFile"//. Its own first version
  had a wrong glob — watching ""<state>/<sid>/"" while the Kit lands at
  ""<state>/egress/<sid>/"" (""runs/<sid>/"" since N13) — and spent three minutes timing out on a file already
  written one directory over. It failed loudly rather than passing, which is the
  only reason that was cheap.
end note
note bottom of SEL
  **proveo owns the install and the version, not the operator.** ""sbx"" is
  pre-GA and its surface moves, so "installed" is not the same as "drivable":
  ""sbx\.MinVersion"" is the surface this build targets, ""Available()"" refuses
  anything older **before the run starts**, and the run refuses with the reason
  named (""sandbox.Unavailable""). Every drift the pin exists for fails deep
  inside a sandbox otherwise, where the operator cannot see it.

  ""sandbox.Ensure"" then offers to close the gap — ""InstallCmd(installed)"" returns
  the //install// line when the CLI is absent and the //upgrade// line when it is
  merely old, because handing "brew install" to someone who installed it
  yesterday is how a version gate becomes advice they follow twice and then
  ignore. The install is **confirmed, never silent**: it mutates the host outside
  proveo's own state (""PROVEO_AUTO_PROVISION"", else a TTY prompt, else
  declined). Declining REFUSES the run since 2026-10-16; it used to fall back.

  **""--print"" provisions nothing.** A dry run is how an operator inspects a
  posture before committing to it; if it could install a package manager's worth
  of software the flag would stop being safe to reach for. ""sandbox.Ready""
  splits on exactly that, and an unready host refuses ""--print"" too.
end note

note bottom of CLI
  **Measured against v0.39, flag by flag, by running it.** What the adapter was
  written for (v0.35) and what the CLI takes differ on every axis that matters,
  and each one failed a real run in turn:

  | workspaces | ""-v host:container"" → **positional** ""PATH[:ro]"" (""--volume"" is ""--cloud"" only) |
  | image | positional → ""-t/--template""; the first positional is the AGENT |
  | agent | **must equal the Kit's ""name""** — "agent name X does not match agent kit name Y" |
  | agent | and must NOT be a built-in — "agent "cursor" is already registered" ⇒ ""proveo-<target>"" |
  | workdir | ""-w"" → **no such flag**; ""PROVEO_WORKDIR"" carries it instead |
  | teardown | ""rm NAME"" → ""rm --force NAME"", or it waits for a confirmation |
  | secrets | ""secret set NAME"" → ""--force"", or a re-run's piped value answers the overwrite prompt and CANCELS the write |

  **The agent is not a separate thing to declare.** A ""kind: sandbox"" Kit defines
  one, and its name is the agent name — an ""sbxAgent:"" manifest field was added
  for this and then refused by sbx, so it is derived from the target instead.

  **It is NAMESPACED, because sbx also refuses to let a Kit shadow a built-in**
  (claude · codex · copilot · cursor · docker-agent · droid · gemini · kiro ·
  opencode · shell). Two defs are named after built-ins, and the refusal is
  silent from the operator's seat: cursor's runs died ~12s in with the session
  gone before the shell. ""sbx\.AgentName"" prefixes rather than checking the list,
  since the list grows and a collision test would pass until it did.

  **Mounts are path-preserving.** A workspace lands inside the VM at its own HOST
  path over virtiofs, the cwd is that path, and the user is the image's own. There
  is no flag to choose a container path, so proveo home travels as a workspace —
  and a bind NESTED under home, the gh config at ""/proveo-home/.config/gh"",
  cannot be reproduced and is dropped.

  //The HOME redirect that used to be described here is gone.// It was written for
  the docker backend, where proveo runs the agent as the HOST's uid; sbx runs its
  own uid-1000 agent and its credential proxy writes ""\.credentials\.json"" into
  that user's home, so repointing HOME ORPHANED the live credential and the agent
  reported "Not logged in" (e2e/ladder_test.go rung 3). ""PROVEO_STATE_HOME""
  publishes the host path instead of moving HOME to it, which is what keeps resume
  state without moving the agent away from its own credential — see
  ""_spec/internal/proveohome/proveo-home-lifecycle\.puml"" and
  ""_spec/internal/sbx/state-sync\.puml"".

  **The image store is crossed locally, per user, and identity is what matters.**
  ""docker save -o <tar>"" then ""template load <tar>"" — it takes a FILE, not stdin,
  so the obvious pipe fails before a byte moves. The tar stages under
  ""os.UserCacheDir()/proveo/sbx-staging"" unless ""$TMPDIR"" is set: a model variant's
  tar is multi-GB and a Linux ""/tmp"" (tmpfs or per-user quota) failed it with
  //disk quota exceeded// (2026-09-26, ""hermes-muse-glimmer""). ""sandbox.disk.dockerVolume""
  sizes the VM's disk, not this host-side write. Not ""~/.proveo"": that is mounted
  into live sandboxes at ""/proveo-home"", so a concurrent agent would see the tar.
  Presence alone is not enough: a
  REBUILT "":latest"" matches by reference and would never reload, which is exactly
  how a sandbox came up with no ""/app"" after the workspace layout was
  standardised and the images rebuilt.

  **Identity comes from a RECEIPT proveo writes, not from the store's IMAGE ID
  column**, because ""sbx create"" re-bakes the template and rewrites that column:
  one create turns ""2ca281d785dc"" into ""5fcb2266417f"" under the same reference.
  Comparing against it mismatches forever after the first run and re-loads a
  multi-GB tar every launch. Full measurements, and the repair that the removed
  reload used to provide by accident, are in
  ""_spec/_experiments/docker-sandbox\.puml"".

  **A workspace must be a DIRECTORY** — "workspace path exists but is not a
  directory". File binds are dropped; since F9 (2026-10-17) the mount plan no
  longer builds the ""\.env"" binds or ""/dev/null"" masks it used to drop.
end note
note bottom of KIT
  **The Kit has TWO schema versions, and ""schemaVersion"" selects the SHAPE of two
  blocks — not just a compatibility marker.**

  | | v1 | v2 (what this package writes) |
  | allowlist | ""network.allowedDomains"" | ""permissions.network.allow"" |
  | entrypoint | a mapping (""{run: […]}"") | a plain list |

  Mixing them fails validation rather than degrading: ""permissions"" under v1 is
  "field not found", and a mapping entrypoint under v2 is "cannot unmarshal !!map
  into []string". v1 still validates with a deprecation warning naming v2.

  //Two earlier claims here were wrong and are corrected by this.// The first said
  ""permissions.network"" //replaces// ""network.allowedDomains"" — taken from the
  published docs, which describe v2 while the binary rejects ""permissions"" at v1.
  The second said ""network.allowedDomains"" was right all along. Both are real;
  the version chooses. ""sbx kit validate"" settled it, and is the only thing that
  could have.

  What v0.39 actually rejected in the shipped Kit was narrower than either claim:
  ""image"" (belongs under ""sandbox"") and ""credentialsEnv"" (replaced by
  ""credentials[]"") are not fields of ""spec\.SpecFile"".

  **""sandbox.entrypoint"" is read from the IMAGE**, not restated per def
  (""docker image inspect … .Config.Entrypoint""): the two would drift the moment a
  def changed its entrypoint, and nothing would notice until a sandbox ran the
  wrong command. It has to be declared because sbx's own agent command would
  otherwise replace it — and that entrypoint is where the seeded guide file, the
  model-alias bridging, the LSP wiring and the subagent set come from.
end note

note bottom of SEC
  **The Kit's credential block IS proveo's broker, declared instead of
  implemented.** ""credentials[].apiKey"" carries ""proxyManaged: true"" so the value
  never enters the VM, and ""inject: [{domain, header, format}]"" names the
  destination and header it may ride — the on-route rule the deleted
  ""internal/broker"" enforced for the docker path, and the convergence the
  experiment note predicted. Only an OWN-AGENT Kit (""kind: sandbox"") declares
  this block; a mixin declares none (_spec/internal/sbx/credential-path\.puml).

  **Derived from the SECRETS being injected, not from the detected providers.**
  Those are different sets: claudecode's ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"" is
  manifest-declared and injected whether or not detection saw it, so keying on
  detection left the Kit silently empty — declaring no brokering while proveo went
  on to broker. A unit test pins it.

  **One entry per service.** anthropic accepts either credential and an operator
  may hold both; declaring the service twice leaves the effective one to map order
  inside sbx. First wins, and the order is meaningful — manifest-declared secrets
  precede detected keys, so the credential the harness announces is the one that
  stands.

  No value is written into the spec: the env var names it, the header says where
  it may go, and the secret travels only over ""sbx secret set"" on stdin.
  ""--token"" would put it on an argv, which the credential boundary forbids.
end note
note bottom of SBX
  **One pinned CLI surface.** ""sbx"" is pre-GA (v0.35), so every flag it takes
  is encoded in exactly four places — ""RunArgs"", ""RemoveArgs"",
  ""SecretSetArgs"", ""WriteKit"" — and callers stay declarative. Drift against
  the tool then costs one edit in one package rather than a sweep through
  ""cmd/proveo"".

  Reference docs:
  | ""https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/install/""        | platform gate + standalone install
  | ""https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/usage/""          | run / exec / cp / ports / policy / secret
  | ""https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/customize/kits/"" | Kit spec.yaml
end note

note bottom of KIT
  **""WriteKit"" returns the directory, not the file.** ""--kit"" takes a
  directory (or a ZIP), while the rendered document is ""«dir»/spec\.yaml"".
  Returning the path to the file invited exactly one bug — handing ""--kit"" a
  ""spec\.yaml"" — so the function returns the value the flag wants and the
  caller has nothing left to get wrong. The rendered file is ""0600"": it holds
  no secret values, but the posture it declares is private.
end note

note bottom of SEL
  **Purity split.** ""sandbox.Spec"" is a pure function of ""sandbox.Input"":
  same input, same Kit + argv + secret list, no side effects — which is what
  makes ""--print"" and the unit tests able to assert the whole invocation
  without a sandbox. Every effect lives in ""sandbox.Run"": render the Kit,
  hydrate the forwarded host env, ""sbx secret set"", exec, then ""sbx rm"" the
  VM, its images and its volumes on the way out.
end note

note right of SEC
  **Two credential routes, one backend.**

  | ""--credentials broker"" (the default) | ""sbx secret set"" host-side; the value never enters argv, the env, or the VM filesystem |
  | ""--credentials forward"" (CLI flag; the form gates it) | a **bare** ""-e NAME"" — ""sbx"" copies the value from the host environment, so it still never rides argv |

  //HISTORY: cursor used to be forward-only// (""egress: [open]"",
  ""credentials: [forward]"" in its manifest), on the reading that its pinned
  TLS left nothing to inject into. The pins were DROPPED 2026-10-16 ("Drop the
  pins"): sbx's secret store knows the cursor service
  (internal/sbx/secret.go), and ""TestCursorBrokersThroughTheSbxStore"" asserts
  the key goes to the store, never a bare ""-e"".
end note

note bottom of SBX
  **A failure with no evidence is itself a diagnosis.**

  The failure handler offers three records of what went wrong, and the first two
  can be legitimately empty at once. An interactive run hands the terminal to the
  child — wrapping stdout would cost the agent its tty — so no tail is taken;
  an agent that dies before its first turn opens no session, so there is no
  transcript to name. What reached the operator was a stopped sandbox and a
  ""kept for diagnosis"" line, which reads as an infrastructure fault and sends
  them into ""sbx exec"" after a cause that was never inside the VM.

  //One of those two emptinesses was a defect rather than a fact.// The transcript
  is written into sandbox-LOCAL volumes, and the copy-out
  (""proveo_sync_state save"") ran on the SUCCESS path only — so
  ""agentTranscript"" searched a host home the failed session had never written
  to, and could never name a transcript however much the agent had said.
  ""saveSandboxState"" now runs on BOTH exits, before the lookup.

  **The sentence that used to stand here was wrong, and both halves cost data.**
  It read: //on a failed run it starts a stopped sandbox and so re-runs the seed's//
  //restore; that is safe for the evidence, because restore is cp -a with no//
  //deletes and the run's own transcript keeps an mtime that postdates startedAt.//

  * ""cp -a"" //has// no deletes and truncates anyway — it opens its destination
    with ""O_TRUNC"". The restart's ""restore"" ran concurrently with this ""save"",
    opposite directions over the same files, and rewrote seven of the operator's
    transcripts short. See ""_spec/internal/sbx/state-sync\.puml"".
  * "an mtime that postdates ""startedAt""" is satisfied by the **restart's own
    artifacts**. One run was handed ""66523790-…jsonl"" as what the agent said:
    zero bytes, created at ""15:12:13.694"" — seventeen seconds after the agent it
    was quoting had died — belonging to no session that ever ran. It won because
    it was the newest ""\.jsonl"" in the home.

  So the window is closed at BOTH ends — ""startedAt"" to ""endedAt"", stamped the
  instant the child returned and before proveo touches anything — and an empty
  file is not evidence. That second rule matters more than it looks: a zero-byte
  transcript satisfied "a transcript exists", which set ""said"" and //suppressed
  the credential hint below//. The run that most needed an explanation got a path
  to an empty file instead.

  **The third record is the run's own log, and the failure line names it.**
  ""PROVEO_HOME/logs"" holds every line the run printed — the resolved posture,
  the credential warnings — and by the time an agent dies those have scrolled off
  a terminal nobody redirected. The macOS run whose login file had blanked tokens
  announced exactly that in its twelfth line, and was diagnosed from scrollback
  that no longer existed. See ""_spec/internal/runlog/run-transcript\.puml"".

  So the empty case is answered rather than left blank. When neither channel
  said anything, proveo asks whether **any** credential reached the agent —
  read off the run's own decision (the rendered env, plus what was written to
  the store, plus a usable login on disk) rather than re-derived, because two
  answers that can disagree will. If none did, it names the variable, the
  command that mints one, and the store step.

  **But the run's own decision cannot see the whole store, and the hint used to
  claim more than it knew.** The credential store is GLOBAL and outlives every
  run, so an entry written last week is injected into a sandbox this run gave
  nothing to. Measured on the failure that exposed it: the stored
  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"" was live — 200 from ""api.anthropic.com/v1/models"" —
  while proveo's own view said no credential had reached the agent. So
  ""sbx secret ls"" is consulted, and when it lists one of the harness's own
  credentials the hint drops the claim and names a suspect instead: //this run
  sent no credential of its own; the agent had only sbx's stored NAME, whose
  value proveo cannot read.//

  **The trap it exists to name:** ""sbx secret ls"" prints the NAME, so an entry
  holding an EMPTY value looks exactly like a populated one, and proveo cannot
  read the value back to tell them apart. An operator who "already set that"
  had, and it was blank.

  Silent whenever a credential DID arrive — a hint that fires on every failure
  trains the operator to skip the one time it was right.

  **The same store is now read BEFORE the run, not only after it.** Reading it
  only from the post-mortem left proveo refusing runs it could have started —
  see ""_spec/_paradigms/credential-boundary\.puml"".
end note

note as Memory #F4F9FB
  **Half the daemon is a SINGLE-sandbox answer.**

  sbx defaults a sandbox to 50% of HOST memory, which cannot bind wherever
  Docker itself runs in a VM: the limit exceeds the machine it sits in, the
  container grows past what the VM has, and the run dies with a bare SIGKILL
  (137) carrying no OOMKilled flag. ""MemoryLimit"" derives the same 50% from the
  daemon's own ""MemTotal"" instead, which is correct on every platform because on
  native Linux ""MemTotal"" //is// host memory.

  **The share is PER-sandbox, and nothing adds them up.** Each limit binds on its
  own, so N sandboxes ask for N×50% of a machine that has 100% and only the host
  notices. Measured: a 48 GiB host whose daemon reports 23.5 GiB gives ""12031m""
  each — four of those is 47 GiB, and ""--cpus"" is never passed, so every
  sandbox also believes it owns all 14 cores.

  So the divisor is stated rather than assumed:
    * ""PROVEO_SBX_INSTANCES"" — how many are meant to coexist. Shares then sum to
      the machine instead of to a multiple of it (n=4 → ""6015m"").
    * ""PROVEO_SBX_MEMORY"" — the cap outright, skipping the derivation entirely.
      It is the only one that works when the daemon cannot be read
      (""8g"" → ""8192m"").

  Neither set leaves the historical half exactly as it was: nobody who set
  nothing gets a behaviour change. A malformed value falls BACK to the
  derivation rather than through to sbx — sbx rejects a bad ""-m"" once the run is
  under way, and reports a flag, never the variable behind it.
end note

note right of SEL
  **No retirement stubs (2026-10-17).** ""PROVEO_SBX""
  off values refused for a day, then "Drop all
  four" removed the refusal: the variable is now
  unread, like ""PROVEO_DIND"".
end note

note as AGENTENV
  **The run builds the agent env itself.** Add-on
  ports, operator name and a host Ollama's
  ""sbx.LocalModelEnv"" fill ""Input.AgentEnv"";
  the docker plan that used to fill it is gone.
end note

note as AGENTENVDROP
  **Proxy and CA variables are dropped here.**
  sbx enforces policy and terminates TLS itself,
  so a bundle naming a proveo proxy would
  black-hole every request the agent makes.
end note

note as ENVGUARD
  **No image reads the workspace .env.** The
  host seeds its values into a new sandbox with
  ""--env-file"" (N7); the guard that once keyed
  on sbx's variables went with the loader.
end note

note as LAUNCHHOLD
  **Startup commands run after the launch.**
  sbx hands the shell over while the kit
  dispatcher still has 15 to 30 seconds to run,
  so everything the agent must already hold is
  resolved host-side into the kit environment.
end note

note as SBXNAME
  **The name is the reuse key, so a test must pin
  it twice.** The golden scrubs the workspace
  fingerprint, which a fresh ""t.TempDir()"" would
  otherwise rewrite each run, and a second
  ""Spec"" call asserts both answers agree.
end note

note bottom of KIT
  **Resolved in ""Spec"", not at write time.**
  Derived inside ""Run"" instead, the dry run was
  silently missing ""--kit"" — the allowlist and
  brokered credentials it carries invisible in
  the one output an operator inspects for them.
end note

note bottom of SEL
  **""--print"" writes the Kit, nothing else.**
  ""--kit"" carries the whole posture and sbx
  refuses a run without it, so printing a path
  nothing wrote fails like an sbx bug. The
  printed argv still needs the secrets stored.
end note

note top of SBX
  **One struct, not eight function variables.**
  Scattered over five files, what the package
  EXECUTES could only be found by grepping for
  ""= func("". It stays a package value: threading
  it would be plumbing for a second impl.
end note

note top of SBX
  **Every daemon call is bounded at 10s.** A
  degraded daemon does not fail, it waits — and
  an unbounded call turns that into a proveo
  that hangs before launch with a blank screen.
end note

note top of SBX
  **The bound is on the CALL, not the process.**
  A context kills what we started; ""Output()""
  waits on the stdout PIPE, which a grandchild
  that inherited it holds open afterwards.
end note

note top of SBX
  Measured: ""sh -c 'sleep 60'"" under a 50ms
  context takes **60s** — shell dies on time,
  ""sleep"" keeps the pipe. ""sleep 60"" started
  directly takes 51ms. Forking hides the hazard.
end note

note top of SBX
  ""WaitDelay"" closes the pipes a grace period
  after the deadline, which is what makes the
  call return. ""docker info"" on a wedged daemon
  is exactly the fork-happy client this is for.
end note

note top of SBX
  **The test for this asserted nothing for a
  while.** It swapped the ""DockerMemTotal"" seam
  for a fake, so no bounded path was under test
  — it measured whether the FAKE returned.
end note

note top of SBX
  And the fake was ""sh -c 'sleep 60'"": it took
  the full minute, the 5s deadline fired, and the
  failure read 'the daemon call is unbounded'
  while pointing at code that had a deadline.
end note

note top of SBX
  **The split is one question:** can this call
  hang while the operator waits, or is it the
  operation they are waiting FOR? Every read-only
  interrogation is bounded; the write path is not.
end note

note top of SBX
  ""Version"", ""SandboxList"", ""SecretList"" and
  ""TemplateList"" had no deadline until
  this pass. ""Version"" is the one that mattered:
  ""sbx.Available()"" hits it before every launch.
end note

note top of SBX
  ""TemplateLoad"", ""TemplateRemove"" and
  ""SecretSet"" must NOT join them: an image load
  runs for minutes, so a 10s bound would abort
  the work rather than protect anyone from it.
end note

note top of SBX
  The wiring is tested through a fake ""sbx"" on
  PATH that exits at once and leaves an orphan
  holding the pipe — a call that stops going
  through ""bounded()"" is the regression to catch.
end note

note as Idle #F4F9FB
  //HISTORY — which backend ran was read from FOUR witnesses.// ""detectBackend""
  keyed on ""backend: docker sandboxes (sbx)"" alone, and a real sweep
  mislabelled an sbx run as docker+egress. With one backend left the helper is
  gone from e2e/ (MEASURED 2026-10-16); the lesson stands for any probe that
  infers a fact from one line of output.

  **Liveness is corroborating, so an unreadable listing reads as alive.**
  ""sbx ls"" is consulted for the sandbox name proveo printed (""proveo-\d+-\d+"");
  a listing that errors reports "running", because failing the test because
  ""sbx ls"" hiccuped would blame the agent for the tool. Listed NOWHERE is the
  failure — that is the sandbox being gone.

  **The probe cleans up after itself.** A kept sandbox outlives the test process,
  so teardown runs ""sbx rm --force <name>"" on the name scraped from the output;
  otherwise the next run trips over it.

  **Every byte sent to the agent is taped, and dumped only on failure.** Three
  theories about this class of failure were built on inference and two collapsed;
  ""PROVEO_TRACE_STDIN"" is the one thing that reports what actually arrived, and
  a passing sweep stays quiet.
end note

@enduml
